    "html": "<p>All numerical examples are original fictional teaching cases in USD, not market benchmarks or promised outcomes. Assumptions are stated beside the calculations; replace them with your own dated evidence.</p><h2 id=\"classify\">Classify the purpose before looking at the payment date</h2><p>Begin with what the resource does. In a manufacturing setting, direct materials, direct production labor and manufacturing overhead belong to the production cost pool. Selling and general administration sit outside that pool. This classification is about the function and recognition of the cost, not whether a bank payment happened this month.</p><p>A factory lease can be a fixed product cost, while sales commission can be a variable period cost. Therefore, “fixed” does not mean “period” and “variable” does not mean “product.” Use <a href=\"/blogs/fixed-vs-variable-costs/\">the cost-behavior guide</a> when the question is how a total changes with activity. Use this guide when the question is where the cost goes before and after a sale.</p><h2 id=\"examples\">Examples of product and period costs</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Classification for a small manufacturer under the stated absorption-costing case</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Cost</th><th scope=\"col\">Classification</th><th scope=\"col\">Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Wood used in finished shelves</th><td>Product — direct materials</td><td>Becomes part of the units produced</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Assembly wages traceable to shelves</th><td>Product — direct labor</td><td>Production work</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Factory lease and production utilities</th><td>Product — manufacturing overhead</td><td>Supports production</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Advertising and sales commission</th><td>Period — selling</td><td>Supports selling rather than production</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">General office bookkeeping</th><td>Period — administration</td><td>General administration</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Shared building rent</th><td>Split using supported use</td><td>Factory and office portions serve different functions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not classify the whole supplier invoice by its label. A single building can contain both production and sales space. Document a reasonable, consistent allocation driver and distinguish abnormal waste or other excluded items under the accounting policy that applies. A planning allocation is not proof that a cost is capitalizable.</p><h2 id=\"case\">Follow a 500-unit batch into inventory and expense</h2><p>A workshop starts with no work in process or finished goods. It completes 500 identical shelves this month. Direct materials cost $6,000; direct labor costs $4,000; manufacturing overhead is $5,000. Assume all $15,000 qualifies for allocation to the completed units, with no abnormal idle capacity, waste, allocation variance or unfinished production. Each shelf therefore carries $30 of product cost.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original one-month case; USD, no taxes or interest</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Checkpoint</th><th scope=\"col\">Calculation</th><th scope=\"col\">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Completed production</th><td>6,000 + 4,000 + 5,000</td><td>15,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Unit product cost</th><td>15,000 / 500</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Cost of goods sold: 400 shelves</th><td>400 × 30</td><td>12,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Ending inventory: 100 shelves</th><td>100 × 30</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Sales revenue</th><td>400 × 60</td><td>24,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Gross profit</th><td>24,000 − 12,000</td><td>12,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Selling and administrative expense</th><td>2,000 + 1,000</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Operating profit</th><td>12,000 − 3,000</td><td>9,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The reconciliation is $15,000 production cost = $12,000 expense through sold units + $3,000 remaining in inventory. The $3,000 selling and administrative expense is separate. Charging the entire production pool to this month and also retaining $3,000 as inventory would count part of the cost twice.</p><h2 id=\"timing\">A later sale changes expense timing, not the original cash payment</h2><p>If the remaining 100 shelves sell next month for $60 each, the base case releases their $3,000 inventory cost to cost of goods sold and records $6,000 sales. This assumes the inventory remains recoverable at its carrying amount and no additional costs or adjustments arise. The inventory release does not require paying the original wood supplier again.</p><p>Keep a separate cash schedule for supplier terms, payroll and customer collections. Product cost can become an expense after its cash payment; a period expense can be accrued before payment. <a href=\"/blogs/accrual-accounting/\">Accrual accounting</a> explains that timing difference. <a href=\"/blogs/how-to-calculate-cost-of-goods-sold/\">The cost-of-goods-sold guide</a> handles the inventory bridge in more detail.</p><h2 id=\"business-model\">Adjust the record for a retailer or service business</h2><p>A retailer usually starts with purchased merchandise and the applicable costs of bringing it to the required condition and location. It does not manufacture the merchandise merely because it relabels its sales spreadsheet. A service business needs a cost model that reflects the actual work, contracts and accounting requirements; the shelf-factory labels should not be pasted onto every consultant invoice.</p><p>For internal job profitability, distinguish traceable job inputs from shared support and selling costs even when external reporting uses different presentation. <a href=\"/blogs/job-order-costing/\">Job-order costing</a> helps trace a specific assignment. <a href=\"/blogs/absorption-vs-variable-costing/\">Absorption versus variable costing</a> addresses the separate question of fixed production overhead under two costing approaches.</p><h2 id=\"review\">Review the classification without using it to hide costs</h2><p>Retain the invoice, business purpose, allocation basis, production quantity and inventory movement. Reconcile opening inventory plus qualifying additions less released cost to closing inventory. Investigate negative quantities, duplicated costs and inconsistent allocations before using the margin.</p><p>Capitalizing a cost postpones expense recognition; it does not make the resource free. Producing unsold stock can tie up cash even while some cost remains on the balance sheet. Review actual demand and stock condition alongside the reported profit. Use the <a href=\"/blogs/small-business-inventory-management/\">inventory management record</a> to reconcile units and purchasing commitments. The decision record should identify who approves classification changes and why the policy remains consistent between periods.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the printable working record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/product-costs-vs-period-costs-worksheet.html\" download>Download the editable HTML worksheet</a> for the completed case and a blank evidence record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit locally, then print or save your completed copy before closing; entries are not uploaded to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method boundaries</h2><p>The references support the named concepts. All case data, comparisons and decision checkpoints are original MyBreakeven examples. Reviewed November 11, 2026. No external professional review is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-managerial-accounting/pages/2-1-distinguish-between-merchandising-manufacturing-and-service-organizations\">OpenStax: business models and product/period cost categories</a></li></ul>",
