    "html": "<p>All numerical examples are original fictional teaching cases in USD, not market benchmarks or promised outcomes. Assumptions are stated beside the calculations; replace them with your own dated evidence.</p><h2 id=\"compare\">Compare the main pricing strategies</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Choose a method for the decision it supports</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Approach</th><th scope=\"col\">Useful evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Main weakness to test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Cost-plus</th><td>Consistent cost base and required markup</td><td>Customers may reject the resulting price</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Value-based</th><td>Evidence of customer outcomes and willingness to pay</td><td>An unsupported value claim does not establish demand</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Competitive</th><td>Comparable scope, service terms and alternatives</td><td>A rival’s price does not reveal its costs</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Penetration</th><td>Introductory-offer cohort and recovery plan</td><td>Discounts can attract low-retention customers</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Tiered or bundled</th><td>Distinct needs, fulfillment costs and capacity</td><td>Customers may trade down or consume more support</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These approaches can be combined. A business can use cost analysis as a floor check, customer interviews to shape the offer and market alternatives as a reality check. The final price still needs an explicit objective, such as contribution over a defined month rather than an unbounded target to “grow.”</p><h2 id=\"inputs\">Define what the customer gets and what fulfillment consumes</h2><p>Write the scope before comparing prices: deliverables, revisions, response time, booking duration, cancellation terms and payment channel. A $250 quote for four hours is not equivalent to a $250 quote with unlimited revisions. Include actual per-sale costs and fees, then identify fixed commitments separately.</p><p>Collect evidence about alternatives and customer choices. Ask what problem the buyer needs solved and which features change that choice. Keep dated interview notes or observed quote outcomes; do not turn one enthusiastic comment into a demand forecast. <a href=\"/blogs/cost-plus-pricing/\">Cost-plus pricing</a> develops the cost-base calculation, while <a href=\"/blogs/calculate-contribution-margin/\">contribution margin</a> checks what each fulfilled sale leaves for fixed costs.</p><h2 id=\"case\">Compare three prices for a capacity-limited service</h2><p>A fictional service has $70 other variable cost per booking, a 3% revenue fee and $0.30 flat fee per booking. Monthly fixed costs are $6,000. Each completed booking takes four productive hours, and 240 hours are available: a maximum of 60 bookings. Candidate volumes below are hypotheses, not estimated elasticities or observed customer behavior.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Same service and cost basis across candidates; USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Price</th><th scope=\"col\">Hypothesized bookings</th><th scope=\"col\">Unit contribution</th><th scope=\"col\">Monthly contribution</th><th scope=\"col\">After fixed costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">200</th><td>60</td><td>123.70</td><td>7,422.00</td><td>1,422.00</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">230</th><td>54</td><td>152.80</td><td>8,251.20</td><td>2,251.20</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">260</th><td>42</td><td>181.90</td><td>7,639.80</td><td>1,639.80</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For example, contribution at $230 is $230 × 0.97 − $70 − $0.30 = $152.80. At 54 bookings it leaves $8,251.20 contribution and $2,251.20 after fixed costs. The $260 candidate has the highest contribution per booking but lower modeled monthly profit because the assumed volume falls. None of these figures establishes the best market price without testing demand.</p><h2 id=\"threshold\">Calculate the volume needed before accepting a price test</h2><p>At $230, whole-booking break-even is ceiling($6,000 / $152.80) = 40 bookings. To match the $1,422 base-case profit from the $200 offer, the $230 offer needs ceiling(($6,000 + $1,422) / $152.80) = 49 bookings. Forty-eight bookings leave only $1,334.40 after fixed costs; 49 leave $1,487.20.</p><p>The theoretical minimum price at full 60-booking capacity is ($6,000 / 60 + $70 + $0.30) / 0.97 = $175.567…; round upward to $175.57. That is a zero-profit capacity calculation under these assumptions, not a recommended selling price. It provides no cushion for downtime, rework, missed payments or a different cost mix. <a href=\"/blogs/break-even-formula-units-dollars/\">The break-even formula guide</a> explains the relationship between volume, fees and target profit.</p><h2 id=\"test\">Run a test that separates price from scope and lead quality</h2><p>Set the proposed price, eligible audience, offer scope and test period before collecting results. Use comparable groups or a controlled rollout where feasible; a busy holiday week and a quiet week are not interchangeable evidence. Record quote acceptance, completed work, refunds, payment fees and actual labor hours. Revenue per lead is useful only if the lead definition stays consistent.</p><p>Assess total contribution, capacity use and customer experience together. A price that raises acceptance but causes delayed delivery or unpaid overtime can reduce the result. Keep any promotional terms clear and apply the relevant consumer, competition and advertising rules for the market; this guide does not establish legal compliance for an individual offer.</p><h2 id=\"decision\">Write a decision rule before seeing the winning number</h2><p>For the fictional $230 test, a useful review would check whether completed demand reaches the 49-booking comparison threshold without exceeding available hours or changing the $70 cost assumption. A pilot with 20 bookings cannot prove a 54-booking full-month forecast by multiplication alone. List remaining uncertainty and a second review date.</p><p>Use <a href=\"/blogs/penetration-pricing/\">penetration pricing</a> when the objective is a deliberately temporary launch offer. Use <a href=\"/blogs/how-many-customers-can-you-lose-after-raising-prices/\">the price-increase volume comparison</a> when evaluating an existing customer base. Review price decisions after changes in supplier cost, service duration, payment channel or customer mix; do not treat the first spreadsheet as a permanent policy.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the printable working record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/pricing-strategies-worksheet.html\" download>Download the editable HTML worksheet</a> for the completed case and a blank evidence record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit locally, then print or save your completed copy before closing; entries are not uploaded to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method boundaries</h2><p>The references support the named concepts. All case data, comparisons and decision checkpoints are original MyBreakeven examples. Reviewed November 11, 2026. No external professional review is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-marketing/pages/12-2-the-five-critical-cs-of-pricing\">OpenStax: cost, customer, competition and pricing context</a></li><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-marketing/pages/12-3-the-five-step-procedure-for-establishing-pricing-policy\">OpenStax: pricing objectives and strategy choices</a></li></ul>",
