    "editorialStatus": "published",
    "image": "/images/title-covers/day6-cost-plus-pricing-20261111.webp",
    "alt": "Workshop owner reviewing product cost notes and a quote beside the Cost-Plus Pricing title.",
    "imageCaption": "AI-created editorial illustration with topic title. Worked numbers in this guide are fictional teaching cases.",
    "html": "<p>All numerical examples are original fictional teaching cases in USD, not market benchmarks or promised outcomes. Assumptions are stated beside the calculations; replace them with your own dated evidence.</p><h2 id=\"base\">Define the cost base and the volume that supports it</h2><p>A quote based only on direct materials is not comparable with one based on materials, labor and allocated support. State whether the base is variable cost, full production cost or a wider internal planning cost. A markup percentage without that definition is incomplete.</p><p>For a fictional workshop, materials are $24 per item, direct labor is $16 and variable production overhead is $5. Its variable production cost is $45 per unit. Monthly fixed production support is $3,000 and selling/administrative commitments are $2,000. At a planning volume of 500 units, the $5,000 fixed pool allocates $10 per unit, giving an internal pricing base of $55. This is a management planning base, not a claim that every $55 belongs in financial-reporting inventory. See <a href=\"/blogs/product-costs-vs-period-costs/\">product versus period costs</a> for that distinction.</p><h2 id=\"formula\">Apply markup and reconcile the resulting margin</h2><p>With a 30% markup on the $55 base, the candidate price is $55 × 1.30 = $71.50 before any fee adjustment. The dollar amount over that base is $16.50, so its base-relative margin is $16.50 / $71.50 = 23.08%, not 30%. A 30% margin on selling price would instead require $55 / 0.70 = $78.571… before fees.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Same $55 internal cost base; USD per unit</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Method</th><th scope=\"col\">Calculation</th><th scope=\"col\">Price / interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">30% markup on cost</th><td>55 × 1.30</td><td>71.50</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Margin from that markup</th><td>16.50 / 71.50</td><td>23.08% before omitted fees</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">30% margin on revenue</th><td>55 / (1 − 0.30)</td><td>78.58 rounded upward, before fees</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a href=\"/blogs/margin-vs-markup/\">Margin versus markup</a> gives the general conversion. Keep the denominator visible whenever a quote or target uses “percent profit.”</p><h2 id=\"fees\">Solve the price when fees are charged on revenue</h2><p>Assume each item is sold in its own transaction and the payment provider charges 3% of selling price plus $0.30 per transaction. To retain the original $16.50 markup amount over the $55 base after fees, solve P × 0.97 − $0.30 − $55 = $16.50. Therefore P = ($71.50 + $0.30) / 0.97 = $74.020618… . A cent-based quote must round upward to $74.03.</p><p>At $74.03, the 3% fee is $2.2209 and the retained amount over the base is $16.5091. At $74.02 it is only $16.4994. The algebra uses unrounded fees; actual processor rounding can change cents and should be reconciled from settlement records. If multiple items share one checkout, allocate the single fixed transaction fee across that order instead of charging it repeatedly to each item.</p><p>If the goal were a 30% margin after those fees over the $55 base, solve P = ($55 + $0.30) / (1 − 0.03 − 0.30) = $82.537313… , or $82.54 upward. The denominator must be positive. This target is materially different from a 30% markup.</p><h2 id=\"volume\">Test the allocated fixed cost against actual volume</h2><p>At the $74.03 quote, contribution before the $5,000 fixed pool is $74.03 × 0.97 − $0.30 − $45 = $26.5091 per item. At 500 sales, total contribution is $13,254.55 and modeled operating profit is $8,254.55. At 300 sales, contribution is $7,952.73 and profit is $2,952.73. The fixed pool remains $5,000 in both scenarios.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Volume sensitivity at one fee-adjusted price; USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Sold units</th><th scope=\"col\">Fixed allocation per sold unit</th><th scope=\"col\">Total contribution</th><th scope=\"col\">Profit after fixed pool</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">500</th><td>10.00</td><td>13,254.55</td><td>8,254.55</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">300</th><td>16.67 displayed</td><td>7,952.73</td><td>2,952.73</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">188</th><td>26.60 displayed</td><td>4,983.71</td><td>−16.29</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">189</th><td>26.46 displayed</td><td>5,010.22</td><td>10.22</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Whole-unit break-even is 189 sales. Do not subtract the original $10 allocation inside contribution and then subtract the full $5,000 again. The pricing allocation helps construct a quote; the period profit bridge uses variable cost per sale and the fixed pool once. <a href=\"/blogs/calculate-contribution-margin/\">Contribution margin</a> provides the bridge.</p><h2 id=\"limits\">A mathematically valid quote still needs a demand test</h2><p>A customer may reject $74.03 if competing alternatives solve the same problem for less. A supplier’s cost increase does not guarantee a buyer will accept the markup. Conversely, a customer may value a differentiated offer above the calculated cost-plus price. Use the quote as one candidate within <a href=\"/blogs/pricing-strategies/\">a broader pricing decision</a>, not evidence of willingness to pay.</p><p>Check labor duration, waste, returns, shipping subsidies, capacity steps and actual payment terms. If a cost is already in the $45 variable amount, do not add it again through a second burden rate. If fixed capacity expands at 600 units, do not extrapolate the same pool indefinitely. Document the volume range for which the quote is valid.</p><h2 id=\"quote\">Keep a quote record that can be audited later</h2><p>Record the cost-base date, included categories, planned volume, fixed pool, markup denominator, fee schedule and rounding rule. State exclusions and quote expiry in the customer offer where relevant. Compare the fulfilled job or item with the original estimate so the next quote reflects real costs.</p><p>Use the worksheet to reconcile both the unit calculation and the total period result. A retained markup amount over an allocated base is not the same as a guaranteed cash surplus: receivables, inventory purchases and tax payments can create a different cash pattern.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the printable working record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/cost-plus-pricing-worksheet.html\" download>Download the editable HTML worksheet</a> for the completed case and a blank evidence record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit locally, then print or save your completed copy before closing; entries are not uploaded to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method boundaries</h2><p>The references support the named concepts. All case data, comparisons and decision checkpoints are original MyBreakeven examples. Reviewed November 11, 2026. No external professional review is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-marketing/pages/12-3-the-five-step-procedure-for-establishing-pricing-policy\">OpenStax: cost-based pricing within pricing policy</a></li></ul>",
