    "imageCaption": "AI-created editorial illustration with topic title. Worked numbers in this guide are fictional teaching cases.",
    "html": "<p>All numerical examples are original fictional teaching cases in USD, not market benchmarks or promised outcomes. Assumptions are stated beside the calculations; replace them with your own dated evidence.</p><h2 id=\"formula\">Use the shortcut only for a level cash-flow pattern</h2><p>For an investment with a single initial outlay and equal positive net annual cash inflows, simple payback = initial investment / annual net cash inflow. A fictional $24,000 outlay with $8,000 a year gives three years. Net cash inflow means additional receipts or cash costs avoided less additional cash spending; it is not gross sales or accounting profit.</p><p>If $8,000 is a forecast profit after depreciation, reconcile that measure to cash before dividing. Exclude financing receipts from project operating benefits. A loan can fund the purchase but does not make the machine recover its cost economically. State the project life: an apparent five-year payback is not achieved by equipment that stops producing cash after year 4.</p><h2 id=\"uneven\">Build a cumulative schedule for uneven cash flows</h2><p>Consider a different fictional project with $30,000 paid now. Its net cash inflows are $9,000 in year 1, $12,000 in year 2, $15,000 in year 3 and $8,000 in year 4. No other cash flows, residual value or taxes are included. The unrounded cumulative schedule identifies the first recovery crossing.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original uneven cash-recovery case; USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Time</th><th scope=\"col\">Net cash flow</th><th scope=\"col\">Cumulative after initial outlay</th><th scope=\"col\">Still unrecovered</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Now</th><td>−30,000</td><td>−30,000</td><td>30,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 1</th><td>9,000</td><td>−21,000</td><td>21,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 2</th><td>12,000</td><td>−9,000</td><td>9,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 3</th><td>15,000</td><td>6,000</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Year 4</th><td>8,000</td><td>14,000</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>At the end of year 2, $9,000 remains. If year-3 cash arrives evenly, the fraction is $9,000 / $15,000 = 0.6 year, giving payback of 2.6 years. That is 7.2 months into year 3 under a 12-month interpolation convention. If all $15,000 instead arrives on the final day of year 3, the cash recovery date is the end of year 3, not 2.6 years.</p><h2 id=\"wrong-average\">Avoid averaging away a cash-flow delay</h2><p>The four-year average inflow is ($9,000 + $12,000 + $15,000 + $8,000) / 4 = $11,000. Dividing $30,000 by $11,000 gives 2.727… years, which does not reproduce the cumulative schedule. The shortcut has hidden when the cash arrives.</p><p>A monthly model is more informative for seasonal or milestone payments. Use the actual planned receipts and payments when available, and report the first period with nonnegative cumulative cash. If annual or quarterly totals are the only evidence, do not invent a precise day of recovery. The uncertainty is in the forecast, not something extra decimal places resolve.</p><h2 id=\"discounted\">Compare discounted payback on a stated time basis</h2><p>For a separate discounted calculation, treat all annual cash flows as year-end receipts and assume a 10% annual discount rate. Discounted cash in year t is CFt / 1.10^t. Keep unrounded values through the cumulative calculation and round only the displayed amounts.</p><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Discounted recovery at an assumed 10%; USD</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">Nominal cash</th><th scope=\"col\">Present value</th><th scope=\"col\">Cumulative PV of inflows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">1</th><td>9,000</td><td>8,181.82</td><td>8,181.82</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2</th><td>12,000</td><td>9,917.36</td><td>18,099.17</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">3</th><td>15,000</td><td>11,269.72</td><td>29,368.90</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">4</th><td>8,000</td><td>5,464.11</td><td>34,833.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The $30,000 outlay is not recovered in discounted terms by the end of year 3. It is recovered at the end of year 4 under the explicit year-end receipt assumption. A conventional interpolation of annual discounted values would give about 3.12 years, but that is an approximation, not the actual receipt date in this schedule. Discounted payback can also fail to occur within the project life even when simple payback does occur.</p><h2 id=\"limits\">Do not confuse recovery with total project value</h2><p>Suppose another project has the same $30,000 outlay and years 1–3 flows, but no year-4 inflow. Its simple payback remains 2.6 years under even year-3 accrual; the first recovery crossing is unchanged. Yet it loses $8,000 nominal later cash and $5,464.11 present value at 10%. A payback ranking alone misses that difference.</p><p>Use <a href=\"/blogs/capital-budgeting/\">capital budgeting</a> to compare the full horizon, NPV and funding constraints. <a href=\"/blogs/cost-benefit-analysis/\">Cost-benefit analysis</a> helps document costs, benefits and noncash considerations. The payback method is especially useful as a liquidity screen when paired with a separate value and risk review.</p><h2 id=\"no-recovery\">Report an unrecovered balance honestly</h2><p>If cumulative inflows never reach the initial outlay during the modeled life, report “not recovered within the forecast horizon” and the remaining amount. Do not return zero, a negative payback period or an annual-average result beyond the project’s supported life. For a zero initial outlay, clarify whether the question is meaningful before interpreting immediate recovery.</p><p>Multiple later outflows require more than a first-crossing check. A project may briefly recover, then need a major overhaul that takes cumulative cash negative again. Show the full schedule and the later deficit. Record the selected timing convention, sources, review date and any cash that is uncertain or conditional before using the result to approve spending.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Use the printable working record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/payback-period-worksheet.html\" download>Download the editable HTML worksheet</a> for the completed case and a blank evidence record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit locally, then print or save your completed copy before closing; entries are not uploaded to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method boundaries</h2><p>The references support the named concepts. All case data, comparisons and decision checkpoints are original MyBreakeven examples. Reviewed November 11, 2026. No external professional review is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-managerial-accounting/pages/11-2-evaluate-the-payback-and-accounting-rate-of-return-in-capital-investment-decisions\">OpenStax: equal and uneven simple cash recovery</a></li><li><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-managerial-accounting/pages/11-3-explain-the-time-value-of-money-and-calculate-present-and-future-values-of-lump-sums-and-annuities\">OpenStax: present-value timing concepts</a></li></ul>",
